00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:23.540
Where do they go?

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.690
How does it get started?

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.360
And then where does
it go from there?

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:27.980
MICHAEL WALLMAN: It
starts with a scoping.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:28.480
Right?

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.540
It starts with a dream--

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:30.926
[ANGELIC CHORUS]

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:33.860
--specifically a GCP.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.703
It starts with a dream of, like,
I want to operate like Google.

00:08:36.703 --> 00:08:37.370
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:38.870
MICHAEL WALLMAN: And
it just starts there.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.370
And then you kind
of work backwards.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:41.460
And what does that mean?

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:46.070
It means being flexible, fast
deployments, able to iterate

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:47.570
very quickly, being secure.

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:48.845
So it starts there.

00:08:48.845 --> 00:08:50.720
And then we kind of work
backwards and figure

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.340
out, how do we actually
make it a reality?

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:54.660
And so it's challenging.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:55.160
Right?

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.120
Because customers are running
more monolithic applications.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:02.360
So they're older, and now they
want to run in containers,

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.720
and microservices, and how
to get the data up there,

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:06.840
et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.757
ANN WALLACE: But I
wouldn't say there's really

00:09:08.757 --> 00:09:12.020
a typical type of engagement
because every customer is

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.200
so unique and brings
different challenges to us.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.640
And it's not always technical.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.410
Sometimes it's more
organizational.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:21.652
But we have to navigate through.

00:09:21.652 --> 00:09:23.610
But sometimes, the
conversations start with us.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.210
Sometimes they start
before us, again working

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:29.733
with maybe solution architecture
team or customer engineers.

00:09:29.733 --> 00:09:32.150
And then we get pulled in when
they want a little bit more

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.157
than those teams can provide.

00:09:34.157 --> 00:09:35.990
And it might just start
with a conversation.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:38.180
Maybe you're at a conference,
and meet up with somebody,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.222
and say, hey, yeah, we
have these sort of things.

00:09:40.222 --> 00:09:42.230
I still feel like
it's pretty organic

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.013
how a lot of these
engagements start.

00:09:44.013 --> 00:09:45.680
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Also
with the packages,

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.320
I mean, Ann, I think
you're 100% correct.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.790
We're constantly trying to
generate reusable content,

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.510
like these reusable packages.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.200
But we found it hard in the
sense of that every customer's

00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:58.700
unique.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:00.020
It's amazing.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:01.790
They're using different
flavors of code,

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.630
and different flavors of
OSs, and different networks.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.320
So I've been in
Cloud a long time.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.070
And there are common
patterns, but it's amazing

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.778
how many different
combinations you see.

00:10:11.778 --> 00:10:13.820
MARK MANDEL: And when
you're doing an engagement,

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.710
what sort of hands-on
engagement are you having there?

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:16.940
I mean, are you sitting
down and building out

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:17.900
architecture diagrams?

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.390
Are you sitting next to
engineers and writing code?

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:20.890
Or--

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:21.640
ANN WALLACE: Yeah.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.700
I think it's a little
bit of everything.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.460
And sometimes the
engagements are short.

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:28.730
So with security, we have
just a security workshop.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:31.520
So we'll go in to a customer
for around three days

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.880
and do a lot of security
deep-dives into our services,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.420
a lot of best practices kind
of tutorials, whiteboarding.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.180
Then we have customers who
want more of the, come in.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.780
Tell us how to move this
from the data center VMs

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.660
to containers in GCP.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:50.700
And that might be coming up with
a technical design document.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.290
So it's writing out a lot of
use cases and diagramming that.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.270
But then there also
might be that case where

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.560
it's like, hey, this is great.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:58.760
You've diagrammed
all this for us

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.700
and delivered this
technical design document.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.540
But we really need
help implementing that.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.120
So then we might have another
engagement where we do that.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.720
We also leverage partners to
help with some of this work.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.820
So if there's a lot
of hands-on keyboard

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:13.580
or they want more
staff on, we tend to,

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.860
instead of having Googlers set
a company for months on end,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.213
we use partners to do that.

00:11:18.213 --> 00:11:19.130
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Yeah.

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:20.180
I would say absolutely.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:22.430
I mean, Google has
never traditionally

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:24.200
been a consulting company.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.370
You don't think of Google
as a consulting company.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.860
So we're definitely
partner-heavy.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:32.270
But we do try to mimic
some of the success Google

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.620
has had internally at
our larger projects.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:38.480
So for example, we have
a TPM that maps to a TL

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:39.848
that maps to partner engineers.

00:11:39.848 --> 00:11:40.640
And we try to run--

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.050
MARK MANDEL: Can you
explain those acronyms just

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:42.990
for those who aren't Googlers?

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:44.073
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Oh, sure.

00:11:44.073 --> 00:11:47.210
Technical Program Manager,
TL is the Technical Lead.

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:49.270
That's how we develop
our products internally.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.080
And so we've had
a lot of success

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.330
mimicking that model at some
of our larger customers.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:56.160
And then we're able
to iterate faster.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.380
Everything is documented.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.090
And we're status reporting well.

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:03.140
And another added layer
of complexity that we see

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:05.670
is that we're not just GCP.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:06.170
Right?

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:07.100
We're G Suite.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:07.940
We're Chrome.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:09.330
We're Android.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.440
So that adds just
another layer of--

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:13.730
I wouldn't say complexity.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:15.950
But when they go
all in on Google,

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:18.462
it's like they get access to
all these different things.

00:12:18.462 --> 00:12:20.420
And it's kind of interesting
how you can really

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.750
transform a business, not
just from a technical stack

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:25.890
but from a people's in-process.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.518
So that's something new that
we've been working on as well.

00:12:28.518 --> 00:12:30.560
AJA HAMMERLY: So you guys
are on the front lines.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:31.977
You're hanging out
with customers.

00:12:31.977 --> 00:12:32.935
You're learning things.

00:12:32.935 --> 00:12:33.890
You're helping folks.

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:35.750
Any trends or
changes that you're

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:37.670
seeing that you find
particularly interesting?

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.170
ANN WALLACE: One
of the trends we're

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.710
seeing a lot is multicloud.

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:44.420
So you have customers who
have been in AWS or Azure

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:45.230
for a while.

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.730
But then they see
things that we're

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:49.230
doing that maybe the other
cloud providers aren't doing,

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.970
that we're just
doing it differently.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.770
So we're seeing customers
coming to us through GKE.

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:57.290
Or we see customers coming to
us for all our ML technologies.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:00.530
But then maybe they continue to
use the other cloud providers

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.180
for other things.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:04.680
And I also, I think,
really in the last year,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:08.338
it's been a big uptick
in GKE or Kubernetes.

00:13:08.338 --> 00:13:10.130
A lot of companies now,
it's like, if we're

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:12.463
moving to the cloud, we just
want to move to containers.

00:13:12.463 --> 00:13:15.030
We don't want to deal
with VMs anymore.

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.760
MICHAEL WALLMAN:
Again, I can concur,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:20.390
being an old-school BSD person.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:23.420
And as an infrastructure lead,
infrastructure historically

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:25.990
is compute network storage.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:29.450
And now they're beginning
to abstract all that away

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:31.640
into this open-source cloud.

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:33.230
I mean, that's what
[INAUDIBLE] says.

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:34.940
We're the open-source cloud.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.610
And therefore, we're
moving away from VMs

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:38.810
and this type of thing.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:43.220
And we're going to containers,
GKE, Istio, service meshes.

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:46.970
And so the long-term
vision, I believe, of GCP

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.800
is to be the open-source cloud.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:50.090
You can run your compute here.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:51.185
You can move it on-prem.

00:13:51.185 --> 00:13:53.100
You can move data around.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.630
So there's a lot more, I guess,
a momentum to that direction.

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:59.330
Companies want to get there
because they kind of don't want

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.720
to be tied to a cloud provider.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.720
They want their freedom.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.460
And I believe GCP is
moving that direction.

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:06.870
We're trying to just open it up.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:08.160
If you want to use GCP, great.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:10.200
You want to run
on-prem, go right ahead.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:10.758
That's great.

00:14:10.758 --> 00:14:13.050
MARK MANDEL: Are you seeing
any particular GCP products

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:14.670
that are super hot right now?

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:17.437
MICHAEL WALLMAN: CSP, and I wish
I knew what the acronym meant.

00:14:17.437 --> 00:14:19.770
ANN WALLACE: Cloud Services
Platform, that's what it is.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.380
MICHAEL WALLMAN: This
is a-- and ironically,

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.210
Eric Brewer, an old
professor of mine,

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:26.610
is kind of leading
the charge here.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.450
It's just the open-source cloud.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:30.480
It's containers, Istio.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.280
And now we're extending
it out in that

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.830
you can extend kind of like
the Kubernetes language

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.050
that's in YAML
deployment language.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.920
And now you can extend it
out to further GCP services.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:43.770
So now I can start defining
Pub/Sub topics and Cloud SQL

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.610
databases all within this
Kubernetes construct.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.798
So I'm super excited to
see that happen and-- yeah.

00:14:48.798 --> 00:14:50.340
MARK MANDEL: Ann,
you seeing anything

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:51.750
in particular with
your customers?

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:53.250
ANN WALLACE: I think
what I'm seeing

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:55.710
is it's not so much hot
services because I've

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.260
been talking to customers who
are really wanting security

00:14:58.260 --> 00:14:59.140
first.

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:01.500
I think the trend is
really to get the security

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.140
teams at the table
from the beginning.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.600
I think a year ago,
even six months ago,

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.060
we'd have customers who
plan all these deployments

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:10.835
and were ready to
go to production.

00:15:10.835 --> 00:15:12.960
And then, all of a sudden,
they get a big red flag.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:13.440
They're like, no.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:14.520
Because the security
teams aren't

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:15.770
brought in from the beginning.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.210
So I'm really seeing a
passion from customers

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:21.097
about getting the security
folks to the table first.

00:15:21.097 --> 00:15:22.680
And coming from a
security background,

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.840
I'm very happy to
see this, and just

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.457
making security part of
the deployment pipeline,

00:15:27.457 --> 00:15:29.790
baking security in from the
beginning so they don't have

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:31.500
to think about it later on.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:32.667
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Absolutely.

00:15:32.667 --> 00:15:36.210
I would say one more thing I'm
seeing a lot is very large data

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.600
migrations.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.900
And I'm not talking about 10
terabytes or even 10 petabytes.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.690
I'm talking a
hundred-plus petabytes.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.420
And so the new challenges
for the infrastructure team

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:51.390
is, how do I even
move this data around?

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:52.200
Right?

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:55.500
I know there are some products
that you come in, and plug

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:58.290
a fiber in, and you're
supposed to close your eyes

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.000
and pray that all
the data gets copied.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.580
But it's just much
more than that

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.100
because you're applying
security policies to the data.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:06.090
You're encrypting it.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.910
And this amount
of data, I think I

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.830
did an analogy in a
presentation saying,

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.060
we're moving so much data
at some of our customers,

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.153
if you were to stack
the hard drives up,

00:16:15.153 --> 00:16:17.070
it would be higher than
like three Golden Gate

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:18.270
bridges or something.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:19.930
It's massive amounts of data.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.930
And I think this is
fairly new that I

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:23.298
haven't experienced to date.

00:16:23.298 --> 00:16:24.840
MARK MANDEL: Why
are they doing that?

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:26.910
MICHAEL WALLMAN:
Servers go end-of-life.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:30.720
They're running these gigantic
Hadoop clusters, NetApp filers.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:31.800
They just go end-of-life.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:34.800
And then now they're faced with,
how do I make use of this data?

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.740
I need to obviously run
MapReduce or parallel

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:38.540
process it.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:41.820
And so I think it's a
combination of end-of-life

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.890
and the new frontier of AI.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:47.125
They want to tap into
Google's AI for the data.

00:16:47.125 --> 00:16:48.750
And the only way to
do that, obviously,

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.680
is to have the data in Cloud.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.380
But again, it's
totally new challenges.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:55.830
I mean, you don't just
rsync or FTP the data up.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:56.897
You know?

00:16:56.897 --> 00:16:59.230
MARK MANDEL: Given that you
work so much with customers,

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.440
you mentioned previously
that you work a lot

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:02.690
with product managers as well.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.829
Is that sort of shaping
product and providing feedback?

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:06.599
What does that internal
stuff look like?

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:07.079
ANN WALLACE: Yeah.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:08.250
So there is a lot of that.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.950
So I work closely with a
lot of the security PMs,

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.310
so Andy Cheng, CJ Johnson, Maya.

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:16.150
So it is giving feedback.

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:18.660
I've also been
contributing to some

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.390
of the PCI work and
compliance work we're doing.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:26.160
And we also do sessions at
Next, the Cloud summits.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.290
We weed internal and what
we call SME academies,

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.490
so it's leveling up a lot of
the folks that are in the field,

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.190
so PSO, the customer
engineers, solution

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.000
architects on security.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.970
So we do this a couple
of times a year,

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:41.130
bring a lot of the security
PMs and other folks

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.050
to talk about roadmaps,
what's going on in the field,

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:46.980
so we're all kind
of on the same page.

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:50.190
And I've been helping build
out some demos for the products

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.040
that we have.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:53.520
So again, that's
partnering with Andy a lot.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:55.720
So yeah, there's a lot of
exciting things going on.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.262
But I really love
the fact that we're

00:17:57.262 --> 00:17:59.190
able to feed back
into the products

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.400
and give input into
what's being built.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.900
MICHAEL WALLMAN: I would say I
have a love-hate relationship

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:05.130
with product managers.

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:06.336
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:08.340
Let me explain.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:09.636
We mostly love each other.

00:18:09.636 --> 00:18:11.940
But back to this.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:14.040
When you're migrating
200 petabytes

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:18.820
to a storage platform, there's
a lot of things to consider.

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:20.610
And so what we do is--

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:24.930
as a TPM, I'm basically
negotiating between product

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:26.930
management and the customer.

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:27.460
Right?

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:29.810
And it's this constant
on in negotiation.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:31.200
And we drive product.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:34.200
Like for example, the
dual-bucket GCS feature,

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:37.870
that I believe now is in beta,
came from a customer saying,

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:41.790
OK, I'm going to have my
100 petabytes in us-east-1.

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:44.868
And if I have a regional outage,
and I have a security event,

00:18:44.868 --> 00:18:46.410
and, I mean, to go
through this data,

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:47.740
like what am I supposed to do?

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:49.770
So there's this new
dual-region bucket that

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:51.610
came out of that requirement.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:53.940
Another example is the
customer said, OK, I

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:57.277
want to process this 100
petabytes in like 20 minutes.

00:18:57.277 --> 00:18:58.110
And you do the math.

00:18:58.110 --> 00:19:02.310
And it's like I need 20
terabits a second of throughput.

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:04.490
That's completely outrageous.

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:07.340
So I was able to work with the
security team and the customer

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:10.350
to back that down to
something reasonable.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:11.600
So that's where the love-hate.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:14.100
I mean, they love me when I'm
negotiating with the customer.

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:15.640
I'm basically
saving the platform.

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.307
But they hate me when I'm
demanding new features.

00:19:18.307 --> 00:19:19.140
But this is ongoing.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:20.970
I mean, any big
customer, it's exciting.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:21.470
Right?

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.252
Product managers always want
to partner with the PSO people.

00:19:24.252 --> 00:19:26.460
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah, but
those features sound amazing.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.820
And it's so cool that they're
driven by real use cases

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:31.580
that we're running into and
solving problems for people

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:32.920
that are on our platform.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:33.770
That's awesome.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:34.460
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Absolutely.

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:36.170
I mean, if you were
to tell me I could

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:39.950
have petabytes and petabytes
of data asynchronously copy

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:41.987
to two locations
at the same time,

00:19:41.987 --> 00:19:43.070
I don't know how they do--

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.638
well, I do know how they do it.

00:19:44.638 --> 00:19:45.930
MARK MANDEL: We have big pipes.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:46.370
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Yeah.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:47.090
It's pretty amazing.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:49.048
AJA HAMMERLY: Very big
pipes and lots of magic.

00:19:49.048 --> 00:19:49.690
[CHIMING]

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:51.770
ANN WALLACE: Our PMs,
they're super busy.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:54.920
They are always very eager
to speak to the customers

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:56.180
and present to customers.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.010
I'm in awe sometimes
how they get

00:19:58.010 --> 00:19:59.360
everything done that they do.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.568
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Just to
add on, I mean, the platform

00:20:01.568 --> 00:20:05.960
is changing so rapidly that,
literally, my full-time job

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:09.320
should just be monitoring
new beta features.

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:13.310
I tried to set up an RSS feed,
but that didn't really work.

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:15.230
I'm trying to figure
out a good way

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:17.190
to digest all this information.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.118
So if any users out there have
a good method of doing this,

00:20:20.118 --> 00:20:20.660
I'm all ears.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:23.200
MARK MANDEL: I hear the podcast
is really good for that.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:25.137
MICHAEL WALLMAN: Yes.

00:20:25.137 --> 00:20:26.220
MARK MANDEL: I don't know.

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.512
MICHAEL WALLMAN: But then
I'd be listening to podcasts,

00:20:28.512 --> 00:20:30.735
like, 24 hours a day

00:20:30.735 --> 00:20:32.360
ANN WALLACE: Well,
if you ran 30 miles,

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:33.920
you could listen to podcasts.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:34.820
AJA HAMMERLY: So
we've heard about some

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:36.890
of the cool and challenging
problems you've run into.

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:38.170
Any others you want
to share with us?

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:39.440
Because I know
everyone loves hearing

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:40.815
about all the
other cool problems

00:20:40.815 --> 00:20:41.792
that other people have.

00:20:41.792 --> 00:20:44.000
MICHAEL WALLMAN: I'll even
speak for Ann on this one.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:44.667
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:20:44.667 --> 00:20:45.710
Oh, no.

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:48.590
MICHAEL WALLMAN: You're getting
ready to roll to production.

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:51.650
You're ready to click the
button and just say go.

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:55.310
And then the CSO walks in
and says, what's going on?

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:56.990
And you're like, oh,
I'm migrating all

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:58.610
our infrastructure to Cloud.

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:00.980
And then he puts a
kibosh on the whole thing

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.690
because, obviously,
it hasn't gone

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.240
through the proper security
reviews, and audits,

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:06.000
and compliance.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:06.500
And--

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:08.458
ANN WALLACE: I mean,
yeah, that's a true story.

00:21:08.458 --> 00:21:10.530
And that's a true story
over and over again.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:13.310
But I think now that we have
the security practice in some

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:16.970
of our sales teams, and our
technical account managers

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:19.970
know that they're trying to
get security at the table

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:22.730
to begin with, and
so we have unblocked

00:21:22.730 --> 00:21:25.280
a couple of these
scenarios just by having

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.260
security folks in the room.

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:28.880
And regardless if
the security folks

00:21:28.880 --> 00:21:30.380
were part of the
infrastructure team

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:33.230
two months ago, but because
they have that security title,

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:34.180
it's helped.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:37.190
And again, it's just
trying to automate security

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:39.650
into the workflow to
begin with and having

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:42.825
it almost seamless, and so you
don't have to think about it.

00:21:42.825 --> 00:21:45.200
MARK MANDEL: Actually, yeah,
you say automating security.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:47.510
Are there any particular tools
or products that you've really

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.140
liked for doing
that kind of stuff?

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:50.220
ANN WALLACE: What
I like right now--

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:52.095
and I always end up
saying this incorrectly--

00:21:52.095 --> 00:21:56.960
but for containers is using
Grafeas and Kritis for signing,

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:58.760
using that with
binary authorization.

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:03.150
So you can set policies to check
for multiple different things.

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:05.780
But for different
vulnerabilities,

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:09.480
vulnerability scores, if they're
high or medium or so forth,

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:12.870
and if those are
found, then Kritis

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:15.343
doesn't sign the
container image.

00:22:15.343 --> 00:22:17.510
And if you go and try to
deploy it, it gets stopped.

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:19.940
So I think that's a
pretty neat tool that we

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:21.270
have out there right now.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:26.270
So binary authorization is in
GCP and Grafeas and Kritis.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.390
And again, I'm sorry if
I said that incorrectly.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:29.300
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
links in the show note.

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:30.050
We'll work it out.

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:31.940
ANN WALLACE: [LAUGHS]
Our open-source

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:33.020
projects that we have.

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:35.020
MICHAEL WALLMAN: You have
your technical issues.

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:36.650
And I think, probably
more importantly,

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:37.817
you have your people issues.

00:22:37.817 --> 00:22:41.060
And it's generally the people
issues that kind of like

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:43.100
provide the roadblock.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:45.800
And so in larger
migrations, you're

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:48.320
navigating emotions,
like I'm almost

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.930
a psychiatrist at
these projects.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.420
I'm coaching them about
their cloud migration

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:56.140
and coaching them about
how to retrain their org.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.890
And it's a lot of pressure.

00:22:57.890 --> 00:22:59.085
You have to retrain people.

00:22:59.085 --> 00:23:00.710
Maybe some people
might lose their jobs

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:02.690
as a result of some
of the migration.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:06.080
So there's a lot of
people aspects to our job.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:07.580
I've got a challenging one.

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:10.620
They've got an architecture
diagram from Miles Ward.

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:13.160
Miles Ward hands them
something beautiful.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:13.820
It's amazing.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:14.660
It's AI.

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:15.860
It's automated.

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:19.818
It's DR. Half the world
could get hit by a meteor,

00:23:19.818 --> 00:23:21.110
and this thing would still run.

00:23:21.110 --> 00:23:23.280
It's like they get handed
this, and they're like,

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:24.300
yes, I want it.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.750
Then you go in there, and
they're running Windows 95.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:27.530
Right?

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.370
So that can be challenging.

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:30.450
They know what they want.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.950
They want the big picture and
the beautiful "Googliness."

00:23:33.950 --> 00:23:36.930
And then it's just kind
of hard to get them there.

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:39.440
And so reality can
be tough sometimes.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:42.860
But we're there to iterate
them and kind of help them out.

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:44.600
MARK MANDEL: So first
of all, hi, Miles.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.180
Second of all, how do you
step people through that?

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:49.160
How do you take
someone from somewhere

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.080
where they're in a less
modern architecture

00:23:51.080 --> 00:23:53.600
and bring them up to speed
in what may be cloud native

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:55.273
or whatever the
buzzword is right now?

00:23:55.273 --> 00:23:57.440
MICHAEL WALLMAN: We're
working on a partner package,

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:59.700
a little package that
basically takes them

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:00.960
the first step to container.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:03.140
So you can run a container
and orchestration

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:04.990
system like Kubernetes.

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:07.810
And it orchestrates
multiple containers,

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:09.950
and load balances, and it
does all that fun stuff.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.880
But you can also run a container
in a one-to-one mapping

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:14.560
to a actual VM.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:17.223
So I'm working on a
package that says, OK, I'm

00:24:17.223 --> 00:24:18.140
going to take your VM.

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:20.573
I'm going to containerize
it, which gets you then

00:24:20.573 --> 00:24:22.240
you can use the tools
that Ann mentioned

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:24.680
to kind of do the security
and get it in a registrar.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:26.680
And then you're going to
deploy these containers

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:28.510
on a one-to-one mapping to GCE.

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:31.240
So now you're containerized,
but you're still

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:33.320
kind of running on a
lift-and-shift mode.

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:35.170
So that's a initial step.

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:38.620
And then security comes in,
and you get all the features.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:41.050
Like GLB, for example, offers--

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:44.000
I believe it has DDoS and
other things like that.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:44.590
Ann.

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:45.490
ANN WALLACE: Actually,
I have something

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:46.690
that's a little interesting.

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:49.090
I don't have an interesting
problem that we've solved.

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:50.590
But I think it's
something different

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:54.940
that PSO does and maybe other
cloud professional services

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:55.523
organizations.

00:24:55.523 --> 00:24:57.191
